tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: Treadwheel cranes and the polyspastos", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Treadwheel, jib, pulley block and lewis holding a stone.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Greece and Rome, pulleys 6th century BCE Greece; Roman treadwheel crane 1st century BCE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Compound pulleys (polyspastos) and a human-powered treadwheel let a few men lift stones of", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "several tonnes; Vitruvius describes the standard three-pulley crane (trispastos) and", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "larger variants.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "A rope runs over multiple sheaves so that the load is shared: with five pulleys, a man", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "exerting 50 kg lifts 250 kg less friction. A treadwheel of 4 to 5 metres diameter turned", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "by men walking inside gives a further 1:20 or so, so a crane could raise about 6 tonnes", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "with four men. Lewis irons gripped the block from a dovetailed hole.", "color": "gray"}
